I think it is important to _emphasize_ that you deal 35 pts. with the
stack of elven daggers, and only 3.75 points more with arrows; this is
a negligible difference in this magnitude of damage so that it's worth
to consider also the other (partly already mentioned) factors. With
daggers it's not necessary to switch weapons, with daggers you can
immediately multi-fire, whether you just used your pick-axe to dig or
decided to keep your powerful melee artifact in your hand. (The latter
(meleeing) is not to diminish if you want to avoid monsters picking up
your thrown weapons and using them or fleeing with them to another
level, or throwing the projectiles at or close to monsters that'd eat
them.) The occasional vanishing arrows (compared to the permanent
daggers) is negligible if the respective factors for reduced breaking
apply. And on the plus side you should consider the weight of daggers
that make arrows very attractive. But then you should also take into
consideration that the OP mentioned already enchanted daggers, while
the arrows are yet unenchanted. So you'd need additional assumptions
about availability of scrolls, or using existing charges to create
sufficiently amounts of scrolls, to make that calculation practical.
(Note, I started my suggestion with "In the given configuration...".)

You call the bias towards arrows a no-brainer? Well... - I'd call it
a no-brainer to focus only on the 10% damage gain. (Probably still a
"no-brainer", but in another sense. :-)

To me it boils down to those questions; what option is more powerful,
what option restricts me, what option requires other ressources that
might be alternatively be spent differently for some gain, what do I
already have, and what threats to I expect. - Your damage consideration
certainly covers the first point.

